</tbody></table>
<p>Examples of defensible groupings: B and F are fractions of one complete 0-to-1 unit; C, D and I all mark halves but have different overall spans; A, E and G have whole-number intervals. These categories overlap: explanations, not a single sorting answer, are teacher-reviewed.</p>
<h2>Andre and Clare: fraction lengths versus fraction points</h2>
<p><strong>Andre:</strong> both intervals from 0 to 1/2 and 1/2 to 1 have <em>length</em> 1/2. <strong>Clare:</strong> the <em>point</em> halfway along the unit is labeled 1/2. Both can make sense when their labels are interpreted correctly.</p>
<h2>Fold and label 5 original unit-fraction cases</h2>
<p>Make each line from zero to one. Count <strong>equal-length spaces</strong>, then put the fraction at the first tick after zero. Teacher checks the physical fold and reasoning.</p>
